From: Domenico D'Arrigo <DCHEM88@hotmail.it>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:24:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 38/42] sb/intel/common: Do not resume from S3 after a power
 button override

A power button override puts the system in S5 whatever state it was
in, and DRAM loses its contents. When the power button is held down
while the system is in S3, the press first wakes the system, which sets
WAK_STS, and the override then turns it off. SLP_TYP is only reset by
RTCRST#, so at the next power on PM1_STS still has WAK_STS, PM1_CNT
still has the S3 sleep type, and romstage takes the S3 resume path. The
resume can only fail: on Haswell with mrc.bin, CBMEM is not found after
the RAM init and the board resets.

The same happens when a resume hangs before romstage clears SLP_TYP and
the user turns the system off with the power button.

Boot normally when PRBTNOR_STS is set, and still clear SLP_TYP.

TEST=Built for lenovo/w541. Not tested on hardware yet.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018n3yuWwDLW63F4vdf7ZFTv
---
 src/southbridge/intel/common/pmclib.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/southbridge/intel/common/pmclib.c b/src/southbridge/intel/common/pmclib.c
index 34a449b2b..638203af6 100644
--- a/src/southbridge/intel/common/pmclib.c
+++ b/src/southbridge/intel/common/pmclib.c
@@ -19,6 +19,19 @@ int southbridge_detect_s3_resume(void)
 {
 	if (platform_is_resuming()) {
 		clear_power_state();
+
+		/*
+		 * A power button override puts the system in S5, so DRAM has
+		 * lost its contents. Holding the power button down in S3
+		 * first wakes the system, which sets WAK_STS, and only
+		 * RTCRST# resets SLP_TYP: the next power on looks like an S3
+		 * resume.
+		 */
+		if (read_pmbase16(PM1_STS) & PRBTNOR_STS) {
+			printk(BIOS_DEBUG, "SB: Power button override after S3, not resuming.\n");
+			return 0;
+		}
+
 		if (!acpi_s3_resume_allowed()) {
 			printk(BIOS_DEBUG, "SB: Resume from S3 detected, but disabled.\n");
 			return 0;
